Correction to the previous note
  That note said gmake GUILE=guile3 was "not sufficient on nexus", implying
  the build was broken here. It was not: the sources are Guile 2.2 compatible
  and gmake passes all four suites under bare guile 2.2.7. The defect was
  narrower and real - the unit recipe ignored $(GUILE) entirely, so the
  variable selected the compiler but not the interpreter that ran the tests.

Tool selection (9c702f0)
  Three defects, none of which failed where the binaries are named as assumed:
  the unit recipe called bare guile rather than $(GUILE); GFORTH was declared
  and never used, frames-diff.sh hardcoding gforth; bin/keel used env -S guile,
  which needs a binary literally named guile. bin/keel is now a /bin/sh
  trampoline preferring guile3, guile-3.0, guile, honouring $GUILE, exporting
  KEEL_HOME so it runs from any directory, exiting 127 with a message when no
  Guile exists. GUILE and GFORTH are exported from the Makefile.

  One self-inflicted bug worth recording: the first trampoline did not work,
  because the explanatory comment in the shell preamble contained the literal
  block-comment terminator. Guile closes the shebang comment at the first one
  it meets, then read the remaining shell text as Scheme (Unbound variable: as).
  Never write that terminator in the preamble prose.

Mutation testing (10f7a1a, 2d690f6)
  tests/mutants.sh: 30 mutants across the three modules, three oracles tried
  in increasing cost (compile, unit suites, gforth differential). First run
  killed 26. Second run, after the fixes below, killed 30 of 30 - 29 by the
  unit suites and one (little-endian memory turned big-endian) only by the
  differential test, which is that test's justification.

  The important finding is not a missing test. The suites were running stale
  bytecode. Guile auto-compiles each suite into ~/.cache/guile/ccache keyed by
  that file's own path and mtime, and the modules export define-inlinable
  procedures (mem@ mem! push! pop! rpush! rpop!) whose bodies are inlined into
  the cached object. Change a module, leave the suite untouched, and the cache
  still holds the previous version of every inlined guard. Deleting the
  return-stack underflow check left gmake unit passing; the same mutation in a
  copy of the tree at another path failed at once, because no cache entry
  existed there. --no-auto-compile does NOT help, Guile still loads the stale
  object; only --fresh-auto-compile invalidates it. The unit recipe now passes
  it, at about a second. This affects ordinary editing, not only the harness.

  Seven coverage gaps closed, each found by a surviving mutant:
  - SRFI-64 test-error does not enforce the error kind in the simple runner,
    so "keel raises keel-error" also passed when a host arithmetic or bytevector
    error escaped a deleted guard. um/mod zero-divisor and overflow, and the @/!
    cell-width bounds, now assert the raised key or message.
  - s32 was probed only at and above the sign bit, where a boundary moved down
    to 2^30 still answers correctly. Now probed at 2^30 and 2^31-1 too.
  - Data-stack underflow was tested with +, which pops twice, so an off-by-one
    guard still tripped on the second pop. Now tested with 0=, a single pop.
  - emit was never given a byte above ASCII, so masking with 127 instead of 255
    changed nothing observable, although the port is ISO-8859-1 for that reason.
  - The step budget had no test at all; the existing runaway test recurses and
    trips the return-stack guard instead.
  - No name anywhere exceeded 14 characters (resolve-leaves), so a length mask
    of 15 rather than 31 was indistinguishable. 16, 31 and 32 now covered.

  The harness undoes mutations with git checkout rather than a saved copy: a
  trap cannot run when the process is killed, and an interrupted first run did
  leave a mutated vm.scm in the tree, recovered from HEAD. It refuses to start
  when the three modules have uncommitted changes, and caps each mutant with a
  timeout.

